LOCAL PATCH (2026-09-27, languages/shells/tcl:B1). Upstream at this rev
rejects valid Tcl, and no upstream branch (main, fixes, update) accepts
it either (measured 2026-09-27). xenolith.patch, beside this file, is the
whole local change to upstream's grammar.js and src/scanner.c:

  - `;` never concatenates a word (scanner.c): `puts hi; set x 1`
  - `(` never concatenates either, so an index attaches to its name
    (scanner.c): `set x $y(z)`, `set a(x) $b(y)`
  - a lone `$` and a `]` are literal inside "...": `expect "$ "`
  - `number` has no sign and an expr function name is an identifier,
    listed below the booleans: `expr {1+2}`, `expr {true && false}`
  - expr functions take 0..n comma-separated args: `max(1,2)`, `rand()`
  - `if`/`elseif` take the noise word `then`; `catch` its options var;
    `try` any number of `on <code>` / `trap <pattern>` handlers

Files regenerated or patched here:

  src/parser.c   generated from the patched grammar.js
  src/scanner.c  upstream's, with the scanner hunk of xenolith.patch

To reproduce: check out the rev above, `patch -p1 < xenolith.patch`,
then `tree-sitter generate` with tree-sitter-cli 0.25.3 (build
2a835ee029dca1c325e6f1c01dbce40396f6123e, the one upstream's own
parser.c names). That CLI regenerates upstream's unpatched parser.c byte
for byte, so the diff in src/parser.c is the patch and nothing else.
Upstream's own test corpus passes after the patch (24 of 24).

Why vendored: the grammar is not published on crates.io (404, measured
2026-09-27, languages/shells/tcl:R194), and a git dependency cannot be
published and breaks the offline build. A refresh is its own commit naming
the new rev, and re-applies xenolith.patch or drops the hunks upstream
took.
